Once I tried my scheme in a large medical way. I had been confined to my
      bed several days with lumbago. My case refused to improve. Finally the
      doctor said,&mdash;

&ldquo;The very words you said, Nancy Taylor. I was as close to you as I am now,
      and I heard them. You may have said hut instead of cabin, but in substance
      it&rsquo;s the same. And you were looking pale, too.&rdquo;

&ldquo;And then,&rdquo; said Mrs. Glossop, &ldquo;what do you think we had better do--let
      Mr. Brown drive the Old People to Nancy Taylor&rsquo;s one at a time, or put
      both of them in the buggy, and him lead the horse?&rdquo;

&ldquo;But who will take care of the other one?&rdquo; said Mrs. Enderby. &ldquo;We musn&rsquo;t
      leave her there in the woods alone, you know&mdash;especially the crazy
      one. There and back is eight miles, you see.&rdquo;

By this time, without any solicitation, they were at the horse&rsquo;s head and
      were beginning to turn him around. The danger was imminent, but Brown
      found his voice again and saved himself. He said&mdash;

&ldquo;Dear, dear, what can we do?&rdquo; said Mrs. Glossop; &ldquo;it is the most mixed-up
      thing that ever was. The fox and the goose and the corn and things&mdash;oh,
      dear, they are nothing to it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The evening chill will be coming on, pretty soon, and those poor old
      burnt-out things will need some kind of covering. Take the lap-robe with
      you, dear.&rdquo;

I cannot understand his willingness to go to Queensland. It is a deep
      puzzle to me. Here is the explanation, from the planter&rsquo;s point of view;
      at least I gather from the missionary&rsquo;s pamphlet that it is the planter&rsquo;s:

&ldquo;We supposed that this was summer; it has the ear-marks of it. You could
      take it to almost any country and deceive people with it. But if it isn&rsquo;t
      summer, what does it lack?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Because you can&rsquo;t. It has been at its worst all night. If you can&rsquo;t
      change it, no harm&rsquo;s done; if you do change it, it&rsquo;s for the better, of
      course. Come.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now you rob him, young man, while I step to my hamper for a fresh bait.
      There&rsquo;s generally something in them worth going for. You&rsquo;ve changed my
      luck, you see. But my goodness, I hope you haven&rsquo;t changed your own.&rdquo;

&ldquo;What use is he? I like that. Don&rsquo;t you know that we can go and report him
      to Government, and you&rsquo;ll get a clean solid eighty shillings bounty? Hard
      cash, you know. What do you think about it now?&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, I don&rsquo;t remember hearing the name before. Now then&mdash;just for
      curiosity&rsquo;s sake&mdash;what has sent you to me on this extraordinary
      errand?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, oh, oh, this is too much! Front! call the police bring the gun&mdash;raise
      the town! All the asylums in Christendom have broken loose in the single
      person of&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, a thousand, thousand pardons! I ought to be ashamed of myself, and I
      am ashamed of myself for thinking that a little bit of a circumstance like
      sending a shark to England to fetch back a market report&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;If you could be a little more definite&mdash;a little less vague&mdash;because
      I am a stranger, and there are many foxes, as you will know even better
      than I, and unless I know which one it is that you desire to identify, and&mdash;&mdash;&rsquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;I knew it! I couldn&rsquo;t hear what he said, but I knew he spoke to you! Do
      you know who it was? It was Lord C., and he is Master of the Buckhounds!
      Tell me&mdash;what do you think of him?&rsquo;

&ldquo;It is true. I was its first President. I was re-elected annually as long
      as its meetings were held in my castle&mdash;Corrigan&mdash;which was five
      years.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No; but my father does. They were schoolboys together. And if you like,
      I&rsquo;ll write and ask father. I know he&rsquo;ll be glad to give it to you for my
      sake.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Come right up on the boiler deck and order the drinks&mdash;it&rsquo;s my
      treat. I&rsquo;m going to tell you all about it. And to-night it&rsquo;s my treat
      again&mdash;and we&rsquo;ll have oysters and a time!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, the pillows, too. Just sand. And not a good quality of sand. It
      packs too hard, and has never been screened. There is too much gravel in
      it. It is like sleeping on nuts.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Plenty of it. There is as good bed-sand in this region as the world can
      furnish. Aerated sand&mdash;and loose; but they won&rsquo;t buy it. They want
      something that will pack solid, and petrify.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I knew that. I was only talking. It&rsquo;s the oddest thing in Australia.
      Another thing: I&rsquo;ve got to get up in the dark, in the morning, to take the
      5 o&rsquo;clock train. Now if the boots&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nobody. You&rsquo;ll call yourself. And you&rsquo;ll light yourself, too. There&rsquo;ll
      not be a light burning in the halls or anywhere. And if you don&rsquo;t carry a
      light, you&rsquo;ll break your neck.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I left him at Ballarat, studying the language. And besides, he had to go
      to Melbourne and get us ready for New Zealand. I&rsquo;ve not tried to pilot
      myself before, and it doesn&rsquo;t look easy.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That settles it. The tone of&mdash;&mdash;oh, you wouldn&rsquo;t ever make it
      in the world. However, that American will point you right, and you&rsquo;ll go.
      You&rsquo;ve got tickets?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, he,&rdquo;&mdash;brightening a little&mdash;&ldquo;he Christian&mdash;Portygee;
      live in Goa; I born Goa; mother not Portygee, mother native-high-caste
      Brahmin&mdash;Coolin Brahmin; highest caste; no other so high caste. I
      high-caste Brahmin, too. Christian, too, same like father; high-caste
      Christian Brahmin, master&mdash;Salvation Army.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, never mind; it was only a random thought; I didn&rsquo;t expect you to
      understand it. How did you get your English; is it an acquirement, or just
      a gift of God?&rdquo;

He did it. But there did not seem to be any short ones, except Mousa&mdash;which
      suggested mouse. It was out of character; it was too soft, too quiet, too
      conservative; it didn&rsquo;t fit his splendid style. I considered, and said&mdash;

&ldquo;Mousa is short enough, but I don&rsquo;t quite like it. It seems
      colorless&mdash;inharmonious&mdash;inadequate;
      and I am sensitive to such things. How do you think Satan would do?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, this is so unusual, that&mdash;that&mdash;well, you see indeed I am
      so unprepared&mdash;I don&rsquo;t quite know what I do mean. Dear me, can&rsquo;t you
      explain? Don&rsquo;t you see that this is a most ex&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Because you won&rsquo;t. Or if you do you can draw on me for a hundred dollars;
      for you will be the first man that has accomplished it in twenty-five
      years. I can&rsquo;t think what Mr. Daly can have been absorbed in. He has
      forgotten a most important detail, and he will feel humiliated in the
      morning when he finds that you tried to get in and couldn&rsquo;t.&rdquo;

At that point we were swept apart by the crowd, somebody detained me with
      a moment&rsquo;s talk, and we did not get together again. But it did not matter;
      I believed he was joking, anyway.

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s no trouble to guess this noble creature&rsquo;s figures: height, three
      feet; length, four feet and three-quarters of an inch; weight, a hundred
      and forty-eight and a quarter.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ye&rsquo;ve hardly missed it the hair&rsquo;s breadth, hardly the shade of a shade,
      your honor! Oh, it&rsquo;s the miraculous eye ye&rsquo;ve got, for the judgmint of a
      dog!&rdquo;

But that was enough. It was easy to understand. It meant: &ldquo;This is
      extraordinary. This is high-handed. I haven&rsquo;t had an experience like this
      before.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No. The guard found a place for me that had been engaged and not
      occupied. I had a whole saloon car all to myself&mdash;oh, quite palatial!
      I never had such luck in my life.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yesterday a visiting party of the British nobility had a picnic at Mount
      Vernon, and in the tomb of Washington they ate their luncheon, sang
      popular songs, played games, and danced waltzes and polkas.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yesterday a visiting party of American pork-millionaires had a picnic in
      Westminster Abbey, and in that sacred place they ate their luncheon, sang
      popular songs, played games, and danced waltzes and polkas.&rdquo;

&ldquo;13. Ich Dien is a title given to Henry VII by the Pope of Rome, when he
      forwarded the Reformation of Cardinal Wolsy to Rome, and for this reason
      he was called Commander of the faith.&rdquo;

We sailed from Calcutta toward the end of March; stopped a day at Madras;
      two or three days in Ceylon; then sailed westward on a long flight for
      Mauritius. From my diary:

A clergyman was asked to guess what was in a box on a table. It was a
      vellum fan painted with the shipwreck, and was &ldquo;one of Virginia&rsquo;s wedding
      gifts.&rdquo;

As to healthiness: I translate from to-day&rsquo;s (April 20) Merchants&rsquo; and
      Planters&rsquo; Gazette, from the article of a regular contributor, &ldquo;Carminge,&rdquo;
      concerning the death of the nephew of a prominent citizen:

They did petition. Also, they issued a Manifesto, whose very first note is
      a bugle-blast of loyalty: &ldquo;We want the establishment of this Republic as a
      true Republic.&rdquo;

From Mrs. Hammond we learn that on the 31st (the day after Johannesburg
      heard of the invasion), &ldquo;The Reform Committee repudiates Dr. Jameson&rsquo;s
      inroad.&rdquo;

Also the populace are in a state of &ldquo;wild enthusiasm,&rdquo; and &ldquo;can scarcely
      be restrained; they want to go out to meet Jameson and bring him in with
      triumphal outcry.&rdquo;

&ldquo;When they gave in they were minus some 20 per cent. of combatants. There
      were 76 casualties. There were 30 men hurt or sick in the wagons. There
      were 27 killed on the spot or mortally wounded.&rdquo;
